  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Rain moves to the south early tonight, but returns\nfor Monday. Flood watch still possible with the support of \nlater model guidance.\n\nThe vast majority of the stronger convection moving through \nCentral and Southern Ohio this evening is aimed SW of our SW\nLaurel Highland zones overnight per the latest HREF and its high\nres members.\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Rain potential continues for much of this week, \nalthough uncertainty remains in the details due to offshore low \ndevelopment possibilities. On the cool side as well.\n\nA fairly strong short wave trough currently over rolls in from\nthe midwest late tonight. That should yield a renewal of \nrainfall in the Laurels. The system will still be working with \n1.5-2.0\" PWATs. But, it will arriving at an even more- stable \ntime of day. The steady east and slightly northward spread of \nprecip will only reinforce the stability (and keep temps chilly)\nMonday. Again, thunder cannot be ruled out, but would likely be\nelevated. It may remain dry (or just patchy drizzle) across the\nnorthern tier for much of the day. Models funnel the best \nmoisture into the eastern half of PA Monday night, easing the \nflooding potential somewhat for the Laurels.\n\n24 hour QPF ENS LPMM in the 12Z Mon-00Z Tue period shows at most\n1-1.5 inches across southern Somerset County with less than 0.25\nof an inch NE of the RT 322 corridor in Central PA and the Lower\nSusq Valley. Another period of slightly more expansive 1-1.5\ninch amounts is expected for Monday night through Tuesday\nmorning, and could enhance the flooding threat across the SW\nthird of the CWA. But with little instability/ heavy rainfall\nrates likely, there's plenty of time at this point to fine tune\nthe area to message for this potential moderately impactful \nevent.\n\nExtremely blocky pattern aloft yields very low confidence in \nsfc features and precip potential/placement beyond Tuesday. Low \npressure tries to develop off shore (along the Gulf Stream) Wed,\nbut may be shunted eastward through the second half of the \nweek. The sure bet this week is for below normal max temps.",
